title Asking whether a binary is current, without building it
prompt The card's own ask in my lane -- whether today's sources would speak the standing key -- claimed as bakery-rye-key-without-building at 20260916.094320 and owed a build.
think The key computation lived inside bridge_to_zig, entangled with four side effects: staging .zig shadows, taking the build lock, deleting the standing receipt, and spawning the toolchain. So the only way to ask whether a binary was current was to attempt a full build, and nobody asked.
think The seam was already there and unnamed. Reading bridge_rye_tree top to bottom, every line above the writeFile READS -- source, permissions, embeds, imports -- and feeds the hasher; every line below WRITES. One bool at exactly that line parts the two without duplicating anything.
think A second key implementation would agree on the day it was written and drift the first time one side was edited alone, and a key reading that drifts reports hit on a binary nobody would ship. So key_only threads through the one existing path rather than beside it, and leg 3 asks for the key BEFORE the build exists so the answer cannot have been copied from a receipt.
think The key reading takes no lock on purpose: a predicate that blocks for the length of somebody else's build is one callers stop reaching for, and reading mid-build can only answer miss, which is the safe direction.
obs The first control proved the wrong claim and a mutation is what said so. Listing the pen before and after, a planted staging walk stood GREEN through every leg -- the defers delete each shadow, so staging-and-tidying is indistinguishable from never-staging. The claim proved was the weaker "leaves nothing behind". Not cosmetic: a shadow is written beside the source in the shared namespace REDS %734 booked, and a reading holding no lock would delete a concurrent build's inputs mid-compile. Leg 2b asks a read-only tree, where a reading that writes cannot succeed at all.
obs A detector is proven armed before it is trusted -- root writes through a read-only directory, so leg 2b creates a probe file first and SKIPS out loud rather than passing for a reason unrelated to the code.
obs Of 16 guards red on the hot pass, three were mine: a compound assert added AFTER running the style check (so the green reading was stale by one edit), and the two pages the baton says a witness-adding round owes. All three repaired green. The other thirteen name peers' files or read over-ceiling with my files removed -- measured by removing them and re-reading, rather than assumed.
obs rye's own build reads unkeyable, and the reason names it exactly: the closure reaches @cImport for getpid. Honest rather than a fault.
rota lap 5091, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; pressed each post and found the one with a hand passing through: a control green against a mutation that should have reddened it.
